  "notes": "Maths: monthly deposits of $200 at the end of each month, 8% effective a year (monthly rate 1.08^(1/12) − 1), from the start age to 65. Worth = 200 × ((1+r)^n − 1) / r, n = 12 × (65 − age), rounded to the nearest $1,000. 18 → $1,126,262 (≈ 9.98× the $112,800 put in); 30 → $428,514; 18 − 30 gap $697,748 ≈ $700,000. Every 9-year wait gives 31-49% of the earlier figure across the table (half or less)."
